! mcp_bootstrap_p157.strap -- "COPY CONTROL WORD", the whole routine,
! printed page 157.  docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF p.164, header READ
! and not assumed: the top line prints "TIME CLOCK 00054041  PUNID
! 11D11MCP   CARD ID IC000001   DIST. OF 3/01   3/01   PAGE NUMBER
! 157".
!
! >>> THE COLUMN-HEADING ROW'S OWN "LOCATION" FIELD IS BLANK. <<<
! Read at 400 dpi off the header strip: the word LOCATION is printed
! at the right-hand end of the heading line and NOTHING follows it.
! That is the expected reading, not a missing one -- line 1 is an
! asterisk banner card that compiles nothing, and
! docs/transcription_workflow.md's rule is that the field is LINE 1'S
! location and is blank when line 1 compiles nothing (printed p.147
! is the discriminating case).  A blank is NOT a zero and it does NOT
! mean the page has no origin; this page's origin is 217766.00 and is
! pinned three ways below.  No digits were read off the dashed rule.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE PAGE IS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THIRTY-FOUR PRINTED LINES on thirty-two cards, thirty of which
! emit bits.  Lines 1-3 are the asterisk box ("COPY CONTROL WORD"),
! line 4 is a printed "SLC,$" that emits nothing, and lines 5-34 are
! thirty instructions back to back.  There is NO DD, NO SYN, NO VF,
! NO XW and NO reservation on this page: every emitting row is an
! instruction, so there is no data-description row anywhere and no
! row prints a trailing "+".
!
! One routine, entered at B CCW R (line 5, 217766.00).  It validates
! and executes a Copy Control Word:
!
!   lines  5-12  VALIDATE THE DESTINATION.  LV,1,$0 picks the
!                destination out of index register 0; BB,S L tests
!                the level bit and goes to B 3MCP if it is on;
!                otherwise L(BU,6),$X1+0.18 pulls the six bit-address
!                bits out of index register 1 and BZRZ sends a
!                non-zero (i.e. not word-aligned) address to B 3ER1;
!                then two KV/BXL and KV/BXH pairs compare $X1 against
!                S TLR+0.32 and S TLR -- the problem program's lower
!                and upper boundaries -- and send an out-of-range
!                address to B 3ER1 as well.
!   lines 13-19  B 3MCP: THE MCP-LEVEL PATH.  BZB on S CH OP leaves
!                for K DONE if the channel is not operating; KVI/BXE
!                waits at K C BUSY if the unit is a disk; two BB's on
!                S SET UP and S SEL leave for B 3FAT; and a KV/BZXE
!                pair compares the file-area activated address
!                against $12 and leaves for B 3FAT if it differs.
!   lines 20-26  B 3TRY: THE COPY ITSELF, UP TO THREE TIMES.  LCI
!                sets index register 6 to a count of 3; the CCW
!                instruction copies 0.0($14) to 0.0($1); LF/SF move
!                the SEOP bit; BZEKJZ leaves for K SUPP if the EKJ
!                indicator is off; CB,6 counts down and goes back to
!                B 3TRY+.32, i.e. to the CCW itself; and the fall-out
!                after three failures is B,KREJT.
!   lines 27-29  B 3ER1: THE ERROR EXIT.  LVI,14,S CCW AD loads the
!                "CW address invalid" code 010010 into index register
!                14, then SIC,S DISIC / B,S DISP.
!   lines 30-31  K DONE: KVI,4,1.32 / BXE,B 3TRY -- if the equipment
!                is a reader, do the CCW anyway.
!   lines 32-34  B 3FAT: LX/SX copy S CCW($13) to 0.0($1) -- the CW
!                has already been copied to the destination address
!                -- and B,K SUPP returns.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THIRTY-TWO CARDS OVER THIRTY-FOUR LINES, AND NOTHING FELL OFF
! EITHER END
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! The card-serial column runs IC000001 (line 1) to IC000032 (line
! 34), read at 600 dpi off the right-hand band.  Line 1's serial is
! also the header's own CARD ID, so nothing fell off the top of the
! render.  Line 34 is followed by blank paper to the foot of the
! form.  The serials are consecutive with TWO printed twice -- one
! card, two statements:
!
!   IC000025  lines 25,26   CB,6,B3TRY+.32     ;  B,KREJT
!   IC000027  lines 28,29   SIC,S DISIC        ;  B,S DISP
!
! 34 lines - 2 doubled = 32 cards = IC000032 - IC000001 + 1.  The
! SIC/branch pair of lines 28-29 is on this page IN FULL and on ONE
! card, so docs/transcription_workflow.md's "A SIC/BRANCH PAIR CAN
! STRADDLE A PAGE BREAK" does not arise here and no row is left out.
!
! >>> AND THE COUNTER CHAIN CLOSES END TO END, WHICH IS A STRONGER
! STATEMENT THAN THE CARD COUNT. <<<  Lines 5-34 are thirty rows of
! printed, known widths (six full words, twenty-four half words).
! Starting at 217766.00 and adding them in order lands on EXACTLY
! the printed location of every one of the other twenty-nine rows,
! including the two octal carries 217777.40 -> 220000.00 and
! 220007.40 -> 220010.00, and leaves the counter at 220011.00.
! There is no slack anywhere in that chain, so no emitting row fell
! out of the MIDDLE of the page either.  All thirty are !CHECKed
! below.
!
! >>> THE SURVEY IS WRONG ABOUT THIS PAGE, TWICE. <<<
! strap/mcp_survey.py read 24 rows and reported a last location of
! "842201.40".  There is no such location on this page and none in
! the volume: it is a misread of line 34's "220010.40" (2->8,
! 2->4, 0->2, 0->0, 1->1 against a drum that opens round glyphs).
! The truth is 30 emitting rows, 217766.00 .. 220010.40, counted off
! the card-serial column as the brief requires.  The survey also
! classed the page MIXED on "1 trailing '+'".  >>> NO ROW ON THIS
! PAGE PRINTS A TRAILING "+" AT ALL. <<<  A "+" in the BINARY OUTPUT
! column is the sign suffix of a data description, and this page has
! no DD, SYN, VF or XW row -- every emitting row is an instruction.
! Checked by eye at 900 dpi over all thirty rows of the binary
! column.  The "+" the survey saw is in the STATEMENT column of line
! 7 ("$X1+0.18") and of line 25 ("B3TRY+.32"), which is address
! arithmetic, not a sign.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE JOINS -- BOTH SIDES CLOSED, NEITHER BY THIS PAGE'S INK
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> THE PAGE PRINTS "SLC,$", SO NO !CHECK BELOW CAN TEST THE
! ORIGIN. <<<  Line 4 is "217766.00 *  SLC,$" -- an SLC that emits
! nothing, whose printed LOCATION is therefore the counter ARRIVING
! from printed p.156.  This deck writes a HARD "SLC,(8)217766.0"
! so it assembles standalone, and a hard SLC overrides the arriving
! counter silently (docs/transcription_workflow.md, "A HARD SLC
! CANNOT DISAGREE WITH THE ARRIVING COUNTER"): every !CHECK here
! would still pass if 217766.00 were wrong.  >>> THE SUBSTITUTION
! THROWS AWAY THE PAGE'S OWN FORWARD-JOIN WITNESS, SO LINE 4'S
! PRINTED LOCATION IS RECORDED HERE AND IN THE LISTING BLOCK EVEN
! THOUGH NO CHECK USES IT: 217766.00. <<<
!
! It is nevertheless fixed three ways, none of which is this page's
! ink:
!
!   (a) NEAR SIDE.  The volume OCR of PDF p.163 -- printed p.156, a
!       DIFFERENT page and therefore admissible -- ends at
!       "217765.00  000006.00+ ... E LAB CW  XW,S CR PM($12),1,$
!        -WRITE LABEL CTL WORD".  An XW is a FULL WORD at 217765.00,
!       so it occupies 217765.00 and 217765.40 and leaves the
!       counter at exactly 217766.00.
!       >>> FOR printed p.156's TRANSCRIBER: that OCR shows a GAP at
!       217764.40. <<<  Its rows run ... 217763.00 (LVI, half),
!       217763.40 (SV, half), 217764.00 (B,Z STEVL, half) -- which
!       leaves the counter at 217764.40 -- and then the XW at
!       217765.00.  An XW is word-aligned, so the assembler would
!       realign silently, but a CNOP or another half word may be
!       printed at 217764.40 that the OCR dropped.  It does not
!       affect THIS page either way (the XW lands on a word boundary
!       and ends at 217766.00 regardless), but p.156's own row count
!       may be one short in the OCR.
!   (b) AN OUTSIDE PAGE PINS THIS PAGE'S ENTRY LABEL TO THE HALF
!       WORD.  Volume OCR line 9140 (a different printed page):
!       "220745.40   217766.10 00   B,B CCW R".  A plain B prints
!       target+.10, so B CCW R is the EVEN half word of 217766 --
!       and B CCW R is line 5 of this page, the first emitting row.
!       So 217766.00 is printed by IBM's assembler somewhere this
!       page cannot reach.
!   (c) FAR SIDE.  The volume OCR of PDF p.165 -- printed p.158,
!       "RELEASE" -- opens with its own asterisk box and then prints
!       "4*  220011.00 *  SLC,$" and "5*  220011.00 ... W REL".
!       Line 34 here is a half word at 220010.40 and so leaves the
!       counter at exactly 220011.00.  They agree.  Independently,
!       OCR line 9142 prints "220746.00  220011.10 00  B,W REL
!       -RELEASE", which puts W REL on the EVEN half word of 220011
!       from a third page.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! GLYPHS -- WHAT WAS SETTLED AND ON WHAT
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! This page opens round glyphs constantly: its own column heading
! prints "LOCATICN" and "BINARY CUTPUT", and every location ending
! in .00 prints as ".CC".  A round glyph in a LOCATION or in an
! OCTAL BINARY field is a 0 with certainty (C is not an octal
! digit), and that is how the whole LOCATION column was read.  The
! glyphs NOT in an octal field were each settled on something else:
!
! (1) LINE 5's "$0" IS A ZERO, AND THE BITS SAY SO.  It prints "$C".
!     The first half word's address field is 000020 octal = word 16,
!     and internal-memory word 16 IS index register 0 (words 16-31
!     are X0-X15).  "$C" is not a register name in the operation set
!     at all.  Measured, varying nothing else:
!         LV,1,$0    000020.02 30  = PRINTED
!         LV,1,$1    000021.02 30
!     and the offset ".02" is J=1 (the J field prints as 2*J for the
!     direct-index value operations; line 11's KV,$X1 prints .02 and
!     printed p.152 line 11's KV,8 prints .20).
!
! (2) "K DONE" IS D-O-N-E AND IT RESTS ON INK PLUS ENGLISH, BECAUSE
!     NOTHING OUTSIDE THIS PAGE REFERENCES IT.  See UNRESOLVED below
!     for exactly how far that goes.  What settles the two round
!     glyphs:
!       * Rendered at 1800 dpi, line 30's NAME prints "K D?NE" with
!         the first glyph carrying a STRAIGHT VERTICAL LEFT STEM and
!         squared top and bottom corners, and the second a bare open
!         ring with no stem.  That is exactly the C/D discriminator
!         of docs/transcription_workflow.md ("the left side"), and
!         the same row's own comment column prints "-IS EQUIPMENT
!         READER" whose D is a known-good instance of the first
!         shape, one row, one drum, four characters away.
!       * Line 13 prints the same name in the STATEMENT column
!         beside "S CH OP($10)", whose O is a known-good O (S CH OP
!         is defined on printed p.99 and carried by four landed
!         decks) and whose "($10)" prints a closed 0 -- the same
!         open ring, same row.
!       * The comment column of the two rows the name governs is
!         English and consistent with DONE: line 30 "-IS EQUIPMENT
!         READER", line 31 "-IF SO, CCW" (i.e. the "done" exit is
!         taken back into the copy when the unit is a reader).
!
! (3) THE BRANCH-ON-BIT TRAILING BYTES ARE READ BY THE ASSEMBLER,
!     NOT BY EYE.  A branch-on-bit's last byte is bits 56-63: the
!     top nibble is part of the literal 111000000 and is always 0,
!     the bottom nibble is mod+cond.  Lines 6, 16 and 17 print "02"
!     and line 13 prints "00", and that pair of values is what
!     spells BB against BZB.  Measured, varying nothing else:
!         BB,SSEL($11),B3FAT    220007.74 02  = PRINTED (line 17)
!         BZB,...               220007.74 00
!         BBZ,...               220007.74 06
!         BZBZ,...              220007.74 04
!         BB1,...               220007.74 0E
!     Both bytes are in the checked set below, so the mnemonics are
!     checked and not asserted.
!
! (4) "BZEKJZ" (line 24) IS THE EKJ INDICATOR, AND BOTH ITS Z's ARE
!     CHECKED.  EKJ is indicator 6 in the operation set, the LEADING
!     Z is "branch when the indicator is OFF" and the TRAILING Z is
!     the branch's CLEAR bit -- docs/transcription_workflow.md is
!     explicit that a trailing Z on a BRANCH is fully visible (0x04
!     of the printed byte) and must NOT be flagged as ink-only; that
!     caveat is about RESERVATIONS, and this page has none.
!     Measured, varying nothing else:
!         BZEKJZ,K SUPP   216242.03 44  = PRINTED
!         BZEKJ,K SUPP    216242.03 40
!         BEKJZ,K SUPP    216242.03 46
!         BEKJ,K SUPP     216242.03 42
!     Printed byte is 44, so BOTH Z's are read off the bits and
!     neither off the ink.  The same two-axis check settles line 8's
!     BZRZ (C0) against BRZ (C2) and line 19's BZXE (C0) against
!     BXE (C2) -- and line 31 prints BXE at C2 on the SAME PAGE, so
!     the C0/C2 pair is printed both ways here, open and closed
!     glyphs in the same character position.
!
! (4a) "CCW" (line 21) IS CHECKED AGAINST ITS WHOLE FAMILY.  The
!     operation lives in the second half word's offset field, which
!     the oracle sees.  Measured, varying nothing else:
!         CCW,0.0($14),0.0($1)   000000.21 01  = PRINTED
!         RD,...                 000000.01 01
!         W,...                  000000.03 01
!         CTL,...                000000.05 01
!         REL,...                000000.23 01
!     and the two indexes are checked by the two bytes (8E is
!     i1 = $14, 01 is i2 = $1).
!
! (5) "LF" IS NOT "L" AND "SF" IS NOT "S" OR "ST" (lines 22, 23).
!     Both are checked through their second half word.  Measured:
!         LF(BU,1),3.24($13)   001000.06 70  = PRINTED
!         L(BU,1),3.24($13)    001000.20 50
!         SF(BU,1),0.24($1)    001000.12 F0  = PRINTED
!         ST(BU,1),0.24($1)    001000.20 D0
!
! (6) INDEX NUMBERS ARE DECIMAL AND THE PAGE PROVES IT TO ITSELF.
!     The low nibble of a VFL full word's first byte is i1, so
!     ($10)->A, ($11)->B, ($12)->C, ($13)->D, ($14)->E.  Lines 13
!     (8A), 16 (8B), 17 (8B), 18 (9C), 21 (8E), 22 (8D), 23 (81),
!     32 (1D) and 33 (11) print exactly that: six different index
!     registers agreeing across nine rows.
!
! (7) THE SAME SYMBOL IS PRINTED WITH AND WITHOUT ITS BLANKS ON THIS
!     ONE PAGE, which is IBM's own assembler saying the blanks are
!     not part of the name:
!         line  8 "B 3ER1"   vs line 10 "B3ER1"
!         line 16 "B 3 FAT"  vs line 17 "B3FAT"  vs line 19 "B 3FAT"
!         line 25 "B3TRY"    vs line 20/31 "B 3TRY"
!         line 17 "SSEL"     vs printed p.152 line 24 "S SEL"
!         line 26 "KREJT"    vs five other pages "K REJT"
!     Note line 16's "B 3 FAT" -- THREE tokens for a six-character
!     name.  All are written closed up below, as every deck since
!     printed p.2 does, and all are within the six-character limit
!     (C22-6531 printed p.10).
!
! (8) THE COMMENT COLUMN IS ENGLISH AND FIXES THREE ABBREVIATIONS
!     THE DRUM OPENED.  Line 15 prints "-IF DISK, WAIT FOR ECP" and
!     the word is EOP (End OP, an indicator name in C22-6531 and the
!     operation printed p.152 line 44 issues as CTL(SEOP)).  Line 22
!     prints "-SECP BIT" and the word is SEOP, the same name.  Line
!     27 prints "-CW DEST ACDR INVALIC" and the words are ADDR and
!     INVALID -- and THAT comment is what identifies the operand:
!     printed p.107 line 13 defines "S CCW AD SYN,(16)1008.0" with
!     the comment "- 8 CW ADDRESS INVALID", at 010010.00, which is
!     the exact 24-bit immediate this line prints.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE ASSEMBLER TOOK EVERY PRINTED FORM ON THIS PAGE
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> NOTHING WAS SUBSTITUTED AND NO ROW WAS LEFT FAILING. <<<
! strap_asm accepted all thirty statements exactly as printed, on
! the first run, with the only change being the house convention of
! closing up the blanks in a symbol (GLYPHS (7)).  In particular it
! took, without complaint:
!   * "$0" and "$X1" and "$X6" -- index registers written three
!     different ways on one page, as an ADDRESS ($0 on line 5 and
!     $X1 on line 7, which resolve to internal-memory words 16 and
!     17) and as a J operand ($X1 on lines 9 and 11, $X6 on line
!     20);
!   * "KVI,$4,.32" on line 14 AND "KVI,4,1.32" on line 30 -- the
!     page prints the J operand of one operation both with and
!     without the "$", and both assemble to the same .51 04;
!   * "BZEKJZ", an indicator branch on EKJ with both modifiers;
!   * "CB,6,B3TRY+.32", a count-and-branch back into the middle of
!     a full word;
!   * "CCW,0.0($14),0.0($1)", the Copy Control Word itself.
! No assembler limitation was hit on this page.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE SECOND ASTERISK, AND WHAT THIS PAGE ADDS TO IT
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Every one of the thirty-four line numbers carries the ordinary
! asterisk ("1*", "34*").  TWO lines carry a SECOND asterisk after
! the LOCATION:
!
!   line  4   217766.00 *   SLC,$                  (emits nothing)
!   line 21   220000.40 *   CCW,0.0($14),0.0($1)   (a full word)
!
! That is the unexplained marker of docs/listing_typography.md 12.
! What this page contributes, and it WIDENS the set rather than
! narrowing it:
!   * line 21 is a FULL-WORD INPUT-OUTPUT instruction on an ODD half
!     word.  Printed p.152's two marked rows were HALF-WORD direct
!     index instructions (KV and SX), and printed p.144 had already
!     disconfirmed "a full word on an odd half word".  So the marker
!     is not tied to a width, to an alignment, or to an operation
!     class -- this page carries a counterexample to each.
!   * line 4 is an "SLC,$" row.  printed p.147 proposed "every
!     printed SLC,$ row carries it" and printed p.151 line 4 refuted
!     it the same afternoon, so this page is a CONFIRMING instance
!     of a hypothesis that is already dead, and it adds nothing.
!   * the pitch is 2 marked lines on a 34-line page, both in the top
!     two-thirds of the form.  >>> NO RULE IS PROPOSED FROM THIS
!     PAGE. <<<
!   * and "the marker falls at a fixed physical distance down the
!     form" is DEAD, measured rather than argued.  PDF pp.158 and
!     164 were rendered with IDENTICAL crop geometry (400 dpi,
!     -x 900 -y 460 -W 2600 -H 560), so a row at the same line
!     number lands at the same physical height in both images.
!     Side by side:
!         printed p.151  4*  217544.00     (no second asterisk)
!         printed p.157  4*  217766.00 *   (second asterisk)
!     Same line number, same height on the form, opposite marking;
!     both are the page's SLC row (line 5 of each repeats line 4's
!     location, which is the shape of a printed "SLC,$").  That one
!     pair also re-confirms printed p.151's refutation of printed
!     p.147's "every printed SLC,$ row carries it", so line 4 here
!     is a confirming instance of a hypothesis that is already dead.
! Recorded as open, with nothing added to it.  It changes no bit:
! both marked rows are in the checked set below and both match
! (line 4 emits nothing, so what is checked is that the row after it
! lands on 217766.00).
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! EXTERNALS -- every one defined by a card on a DIFFERENT page, and
! every one already carried by a LANDED deck at the same value
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! ZERO TAUTOLOGICAL ROWS.  This page's own cards sit at OCR lines
! ~8160-8192 of docs/ocr/MCP_Mar63_001-249.txt; every witness cited
! below is a different printed page, so none of them is this page
! reading itself.
!
! S COM RG 215001.00  printed p.103 line 8 DEFINES it, "S COM RG
!                     XW,(8)520000."  Carried only so S L can be
!                     written the way p.103 writes it.
SCOMRG  XW, (8)520000.
! S L      215001.04  printed p.103 line 14 DEFINES it,
!                     "S L SYN(BU,1),S COM RG+0.04  -LEVEL OF
!                     INSTRUCTION"; decks p.117, p.123 and p.126
!                     carry it.  Volume OCR line 6126 is a DIFFERENT
!                     page printing "CM1111(BU,1),S L" against
!                     "215001.04 80".  Line 6 here is a
!                     branch-on-bit whose B field is a full 24-bit
!                     BIT address, so this page pins S L TO THE BIT.
SL      SYN(BU,1), (8)215001.04
! S TLR    215230.00  printed p.116 line 13 DEFINES it, "S TLR
!                     DRZ(BU,64),29"; decks p.116, p.117, p.118,
!                     p.122, p.123 and p.124 carry it.  Lines 9 and
!                     11 here are KV's whose address field is a full
!                     24-bit BIT address, so they pin it TO THE BIT
!                     -- and through TWO different offsets (+0.32
!                     prints .42, bare prints .02), which is what
!                     makes the reading self-checking.
STLR    SYN, (8)215230.0
! S CH OP  000000.32  printed p.99 "-CHAN OPERATING"; four landed
!                     decks (p.123, p.152 and others) carry 0.26
!                     (= 0o32).  Line 13 pins the bit.
SCHOP   SYN(BU,1), 0.26
! S SET UP 000000.37  printed p.99 line 25 DEFINES it, "S SET UP
!                     SYN(BU,1),0.31  -PSEUDO-OP SET-UP, UNIT
!                     STATUS"; decks p.122 and p.127 carry 0.31
!                     (= 0o37).  Line 16 pins the bit.
SSETUP  SYN(BU,1), 0.31
! S SEL    000000.47  printed p.99 line 32 "-SELECT FILE STATUS";
!                     five landed decks carry 0.39 (= 0o47).  Line
!                     17 pins the bit.
SSEL    SYN(BU,1), 0.39
! S FI AAC 000003.00  printed p.101 line 13 DEFINES it, "S FI AAC
!                     SYN(BU,18),3.0  -FILE AREA ACTIVATED ADDRESS",
!                     and Appendix A printed p.269 spells it SFIAAC.
!                     Line 18 pins the bit (its ".32" is J=13 alone,
!                     2*13 = 26 = 0o32, with no bit offset).
SFIAAC  SYN(BU,18), 3.0
! S CCW    000004.00  printed p.102 line 15 DEFINES it, "S CCW
!                     SYN(BU,64),4.0  -COPIED CONTROL WORD"; decks
!                     p.102, p.122, p.123, p.124, p.126 and p.127
!                     carry it.  Line 32 is an LX, so it pins only
!                     the half word.
SCCW    SYN(BU,64), 4.0
! S CCW AD 010010.00  printed p.107 line 13 DEFINES it, "S CCW AD
!                     SYN,(16)1008.0  - 8 CW ADDRESS INVALID",
!                     printed there as "010010.00+".  Line 27 here
!                     is an LVI, so it pins only the HALF WORD (bit
!                     32 shows, the bits below it do not) -- see
!                     WHAT THE CHECKS DO NOT SHOW.  >>> printed
!                     p.107's own deck records this name as
!                     UNRESOLVED between SCCWAD / SCOWAD / SOCWAD;
!                     this page prints it "S CCW AD" with the
!                     comment "-CW DEST ADDR INVALID", which is a
!                     SECOND, INDEPENDENT page reading the first two
!                     round glyphs as C and C.  That is evidence,
!                     not proof; see UNRESOLVED. <<<
SCCWAD  SYN, (8)010010.0
! S DISIC  217451.40  deck p.103 carries it, from printed pp.119,
!                     169, 173 and 193 all printing "SIC,S DISIC"
!                     against "217451.40 80"; volume OCR line 6234
!                     is one of them.  Line 28 here is a SIC, which
!                     prints the BARE 24-bit address with no
!                     operation bits, so this row pins S DISIC TO
!                     THE BIT.
SDISIC  SYN, (8)217451.40
! S DISP   217372.00  decks p.119 and p.120 carry it; eight other
!                     printed pages print "B,S DISP" against
!                     "217372.10 00" (volume OCR 6232, 6244, 6253,
!                     8721, 9294, 9375, 9403).  Line 29 is a plain
!                     branch: HALF WORD only.
SDISP   SYN, (8)217372.0
! K SUPP   216242.00  printed p.128 line 3 is the label row,
!                     "K SUPP  NOP,K COMM R"; eleven landed decks
!                     carry it.  Lines 24 and 34 pin only the half
!                     word.
KSUPP   SYN, (8)216242.0
! K C BUSY 221065.00  the volume prints the LABEL ROW on another
!                     page: OCR line 9290, "21* 221065.00 ... 0D
!                     K C BUSY  V-I,...".  Line 15's BXE pins the
!                     half word.
KCBUSY  SYN, (8)221065.0
! K REJT   221300.00  the volume prints the LABEL ROW on another
!                     page: OCR line 9561, "43* 221300.00 003406.35
!                     K REJT  LVI,14,S REJ  -I/O COMMAND REJECTED";
!                     deck p.125 carries it, and six other pages
!                     print "B,K REJT" against "221300.10 00".
!                     Line 26 is a plain branch: HALF WORD only.
KREJT   SYN, (8)221300.0

